<crash-analysis>
The crash is caused by a heap-buffer-overflow in the `json_next_token` function in `quickjs.c`. The code dereferences the `p` pointer before checking if it is within the bounds of the input buffer. This happens when the input JSON string is malformed and ends abruptly.

The fix is to move the check `if (p >= s->buf_end)` before the `c = *p;` line. This will ensure that the code doesn't read past the end of the buffer.

```c
// quickjs.c:22125
// before
c = *p;
switch(c) {
case 0:
if (p >= s->buf_end) {
s->token.val = TOK_EOF;
break;
}
/* fall thru */

// after
if (p >= s->buf_end) {
s->token.val = TOK_EOF;
break;
}
c = *p;
switch(c) {
case 0:
/* fall thru */
```
</crash-analysis>

